# FLARE vs {{ other }} concordance

**What this is.** For each ancestry, two stats describing how FLARE's
per-sample global proportion lines up against {{ other }}'s per-sample
proportion for that ancestry. **Pearson r** measures rank co-variation
(do they move together?). **Lin's CCC** additionally measures absolute
agreement (do they lie on y=x?). A systematic offset (FLARE
consistently calling 10 percentage points higher than {{ other }})
leaves r near 1 but tanks CCC.
{% if src == "rye" %}
Rye is used here because both FLARE and Rye emit soft proportions on a
shared 5-label superpop basis (eur, eas, amr, afr, sas); the comparison
is apples-to-apples without any categorical-superpop penalty.
{% else %}
RF emits soft posteriors on SP6 (afr, amr, eas, eur, mid, sas). The
per-ancestry r and CCC are computed on the 5-label SP5 intersection;
MID is RF-only and lives in the confusion section. The acceptance
thresholds (r >= 0.95 and CCC >= 0.90) are the same as for Rye, but
expect lower scores: RF is a hard-call classifier whose soft
probabilities are class-confidence, while FLARE's proportions are
genome-fractions. The same numbers mean different things, so
agreement is naturally bounded below 1.
{% endif %}

**How to read this chart.** Each row is one ancestry, present in the
cohort. The row is partitioned into three non-overlapping lanes
(top -> bottom):

- **Half-violin** (above): KDE of the per-`(cluster, chrom)` values,
  shaded by ancestry. Wider regions = more density.
- **Cohort bar** (middle): the cohort-pooled, n_samples-weighted
  value, with the printed number to the right.
- **Sina rain** (below): one dot per `(cluster, chrom)` row that
  passed the mu-gate (`cluster_mu >= 0.01`). Vertical jitter is
  proportional to local KDE density so dense clusters of values
  fan out and sparse tails collapse to a thin line.

The dashed vertical line is the pass threshold (0.95 for r, 0.90 for
CCC). Rows with `cluster_mu < 0.01` are degenerate and excluded.

